# Application contracts

Match existing module patterns. Prefer Identity-style one-type-per-file over large `*Models.cs` bags.

## File layout

- **One public type per file**, named after the type (`AuditLogListQuery.cs`, `AuditLogEntryDto.cs`, `AppendAuditLogCommand.cs`, `CreateBrandCommandValidator.cs`).
- Do not put multiple queries/DTOs/commands/validators/interfaces in one bag file (`*Models.cs`, `*CommandValidators.cs`, multi-interface files).
- Keep them under the feature folder (e.g. `Audit.Application/AuditLogs/`); validators under `{Feature}/Validators/`.
- Prefer a feature service + `I{Feature}Service`; do not add MediatR handlers unless the module already uses them.

## Queries

- List/filter endpoints use `sealed class {Feature}ListQuery : PaginatedRequest` with extra filter properties.
- Do **not** duplicate the same filters on a second query type (e.g. no separate `*SummaryQuery` that copies list filters). Reuse `{Feature}ListQuery` for summary/stats endpoints; ignore pagination when unused.
- Query types use settable properties for ASP.NET `[FromQuery]` binding.

## DTOs and commands

- Use `sealed record` for DTOs and commands.
- Use `DateTimeOffset` for timestamps (not ISO `string`). JSON serialization already emits ISO-8601.
- Name reads `*Dto`, writes `Create*Command` / `Update*Command` / action-specific `*Command`.

## Example

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — AuditLogListQuery.cs (alone)
public sealed class AuditLogListQuery : PaginatedRequest
{
    public string? Entity { get; set; }
    public DateTimeOffset? From { get; set; }
}

// ✅ GOOD — AuditLogEntryDto.cs (alone)
public sealed record AuditLogEntryDto(Guid Id, DateTimeOffset Timestamp, string Action);

// ❌ BAD — multiple types in one Models.cs, duplicated filters, string timestamps
public sealed class AuditLogSummaryQuery { public string? Entity { get; set; } }
public sealed record AuditLogEntryDto(Guid Id, string Timestamp, string Action);
```
